      "Monte Albán was one of the first cities in Mesoamerica, and one of the earliest to use writing and a calendar. Among its most mysterious monuments are the Danzantes, stone slabs carved with figures in contorted poses, once thought to be dancers but now believed to depict captured and sacrificed enemies. The site was declared a UNESCO World Heritage Site, together with the historic centre of Oaxaca, in 1987.",
      "Every year on 23 December, Oaxaca celebrates the Night of the Radishes, a festival that began in 1897, in which local farmers and artists carve giant radishes into elaborate scenes of nativities, saints, dancers and animals. The radishes, specially grown for the event, can be very large and oddly shaped. Thousands of people line up in the main square to see them before they wilt.",
