      "Na Bolom, a house in San Cristóbal, was the home of the Danish archaeologist Frans Blom and his Swiss wife, Gertrude Duby Blom, a journalist and photographer, from 1950. They devoted their lives to studying and defending the Lacandon Maya of the Chiapas rainforest and campaigning against the destruction of the jungle. Their house is now a museum and research centre, and guests can stay in its rooms.",
      "In the Tzotzil village of San Juan Chamula, just outside San Cristóbal, the church is a centre of a unique blend of Catholic and Maya religion. There are no pews; the floor is covered with pine needles, and families kneel among hundreds of candles, praying in Tzotzil, sometimes with offerings of eggs, chickens and soft drinks. Taking photographs inside is strictly forbidden.",
      "The Zapatistas' autonomous communities, known as caracoles, run their own schools, health clinics and courts, independent of the Mexican government. The movement inspired activists around the world with its rejection of globalisation and its slogans, such as a world where many worlds fit. Its relationship with the government remains tense.",
      "San Cristóbal's market is one of the busiest in southern Mexico, where Maya traders from the surrounding villages sell vegetables, flowers, textiles and pottery. The weavers of the highlands produce brightly coloured fabrics, and each village has its own patterns, worn proudly by its women. The city also has museums of Maya medicine, textiles and amber, a fossil resin found in Chiapas.",
